National Bank of Cambodia provides an opportunity for citizens to express their concerns about the use of microfinance institutions
(Battambang): The National Bank of Cambodia (NBC) is organizing an outreach workshop on "Microfinance Sector in Cambodia", which was organized to provide participants with an opportunity to express their concerns about the use of services by microfinance institutions in Cambodia. Its basis for working together to make this sector more active, strong and free from various risks.
The dissemination workshop on microfinance in Cambodia was held in Sangke district, Battambang province and on August 27, 2019 with the participation of Mr. Sou Sannisith, Deputy Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia, Mr. Rath Sovannarak, Director General of Inspection. National Bank of Cambodia, National Bank of Cambodia officials and many people.
Mr. Rath Sovannarak said that the banking sector, which has the role of raising capital to support the development of the national economy to grow both in scope and scope of operations, in which the microfinance sector has also actively contributed to improving the lives of people through Increased access to financial services such as credit and special savings has been provided to remote rural areas.
Mr. Sou Sannisith said that all banks and microfinance institutions have a role to comply with the laws and regulations in force and the principles of consumer protection, and must be careful in their credit operations.
He said that overall, all institutions are private institutions seeking profit for their financial sustainability to continue to provide loans to the people. Therefore, citizens who borrow money from a bank and microfinance institution must be obliged to repay the loan to that institution.
He added that a stronger financial system is when borrowers and lenders work together to protect themselves from microfinance-related problems through careful implementation or decision-making, as the saying goes. "Protect each other, keep each other for long-term sustainability," he said.
Mr. Sannisith also urged stakeholders to cooperate and make efforts to develop the microfinance sector in the province to grow continuously to contribute to reducing poverty. In addition, we will continue to prevent any fraudulent activities of crooks who want to borrow or illegally lend money illegally.
It should be noted that the microfinance sector in Cambodia was established in the 1980s and developed rapidly in the early 1990s by providing microfinance activities to people in rural areas in order to provide both people. That capital for creating or increasing their business. Samdech Techo Hun Sen Issues a Sub-Decree on the Transfer of Additional Resources from the State Budget to Commune / Sangkat Administration for Local Development Expenditure
