(Phnom Penh): To support the sustainable development of Cambodia's civil aviation sector and contribute to the economic development of Cambodia, especially human resources in air transport in Cambodia, Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA-KOICA) Continue to support Cambodia by providing new generators with a maximum capacity of 510KVA and improving the garden water system at the Cambodia Civil Aviation Training Center (Cambodia-CATC).
Activities to improve the garden water system in this building include 1) - Installation of high pressure water compressor to supply water system in the garden. 2) - Installation of new water pipes to support the water flow system in the garden.
The purpose of these activities is to benefit students and teachers in helping to maintain a comfortable learning environment through the natural colors of grass, flowers and trees, and the garden relieves stress from teaching and learning. In addition, it can be a learning space where all participants can participate in communication and cooperation.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen calls for 8 points to continue to improve literacy in Cambodia
In the context of the Sustainable Development Goals 2030 and to link literacy to vocational skills, especially to achieve the goal of transforming pure labor into skilled workers in line with Cambodia's "new growth strategy" Samdech Techo Hun Sen called on national, international, community and all citizens to pay more attention to literacy as well as education, both in and out of the system.
In this spirit, in order to continue to contribute to the promotion of literacy in Cambodia more effectively, Samdech called for the following 8 points:
First, the relevant ministries and institutions must prepare the necessary policies, legal frameworks, letters, norms and mechanisms to lead, manage and implement effectively and qualitatively, ensuring acceptable results in providing lifelong learning for all citizens and consistent. To the new context of the Sustainable Development Goals.
Second, all ministries, institutions, educational institutions, study centers and pagodas must develop a lifelong educational program, both literate and professional, to be more diverse and flexible to meet the needs of students to provide lifelong educational services. In-system education services, both non-formal education and informal education services for all citizens.
Third, local authorities at all levels must pay more attention to support the development of learning center infrastructure and encourage people who have not studied or dropped out of school to have the opportunity to study. New non-formal education classes in all forms, especially to make it easier for people with disabilities to have access to education.
Fourth, all media, including social media, radio, television, newspapers, magazines and newsletters, public and private, please help spread the word about the power of literacy to the people to have a broader understanding, that is, "Literacy linked Occupation skills.
Sixth, pagodas and temples must encourage the movement of monks, priests, nuns and nuns to participate in the literacy program linked to the business even more by educating about the benefits of learning about morality and promoting the culture of living as citizens. Global.
Seventh, development partners and NGOs need to increase cooperation and strengthen partnerships by supporting non-formal education programs, especially literacy programs linked to vocational education.
(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Health, Prof. Mam Bun Heng, stated that the Ministry and the Institut Pasteur du Cambodge are jointly committed to expand rabies prevention centers in provinces such as Stung Treng, Svay Rieng or Kampot and almost nationwide. That we can do to provide convenience and better services to the people.
