Mr. Ith Sam Heng recommends that Southeast Asian youth learn about the progress of each country and share the development of Cambodia.
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of August 27, 2019, Mr. Ith Sam Heng, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training, made a number of good recommendations to the above youths in order to promote them. Let them strive to study the progress of each country to bring good experiences to contribute to the development of their country and share the development of Cambodia's economic growth to those countries.
During the meeting held at the Ministry of Labor, the Minister advised that "Cambodian youth must strive to learn more about general knowledge related to regional and international institutions to which Cambodia is a member, as well as the benefits of cooperation with Those countries for Cambodia.
He said that all young people who are delegates must pay attention and learn from the situation of other countries and the development of that country to bring good points to develop their country, especially all young people must be A good guide as a diplomat represents Cambodia by maintaining the honor, values and culture of the nation by striving to promote the culture, tourism and national identity, as well as maintaining good relations with the youth of other countries.
He called on them to be loving, understanding, tolerant, united and work well as a team to bring honor to our motherland, as the Khmer proverb says, in the house of another mother in the same mother forest.
According to Mr. Nay Semnaroth, Vice President of the Southeast Asian Youth Ship Association of Cambodia, all the youth delegations are working hard to learn and strengthen friendships, share the culture of each country to help the society.
He said that this is the 46th program for 2019, which lasts for 51 days (from October 24 to December 13, 2019), with ships will dock in five countries: Japan, Vietnam, Singapore. Myanmar and Malaysia. Prior to the event, all Cambodian youth delegates received three months of pre-departure training (starting August 10, 2019).
Please note that every year, this program always brings together more than 300 young people from ASEAN and Japan. As the ship docks in the ports of each country, all the youth delegates will visit the institutions of that country, especially the opportunity to meet with the head of government as well as the princes and princesses of the country. Japan, too.
In the wake of this concern, the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking in collaboration with Action for Children (APLE) on the morning of August 27, 2019 organized a workshop on "Prevention and response to child sex offenses in Tourism "where the ceremony was held at the Ministry of Interior.
The workshop aims to raise awareness of child sexual exploitation in tourism and to seek input to identify key strategies and activities to combat human trafficking and child sexual abuse.
Speaking on the occasion, Ms. Chou Bun Eng emphasized that in the past, in order to prevent and prevent this problem, the Ministry of Interior has also set up a department, the Department of Technology Crimes, but while increasing the legal mechanism, the Ministry also called on stakeholders, especially guardians. More attention must be paid.
"We want to make the most of this technology, rather than using it as a tool for exploitation or child abuse, as we already know," he said. "Little children do not know, and now what seems to be abuse seems to be getting worse, with child victims getting worse because of the more brutal treatment of children."
Responding to this issue, Ms. Chou Bun Eng claimed that for the Anti-Trafficking Committee can not do this work alone, that is, all relevant institutions must work together with other development partners to fill the gaps to reach Eliminate child exploitation.
Sexual exploitation of children in tourism is becoming a global problem and a form of abuse that is having a devastating effect on children. Many research reports show that perpetrators prefer to travel to developing countries, countries with weak child protection systems, and countries with vulnerable communities where families are more concerned about their own interests and money than child safety.
So far, however, the Anti-Human Trafficking Committee has identified five provinces that are targeting the need to increase protection for vulnerable children, including Kampot, Siem Reap, Sihanoukville and Phnom Penh. OCIC would like to thank and encourage the police who intervened to put out the fire in the warehouse below The Olympia Mall in early August.
