Legal Awareness: Mechanisms for Land Dispute Resolution in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia pays great attention to respecting human rights, especially the privacy rights of the people.
In fact, during 1992, Cambodia implemented a land law, which specified the acquisition of land ownership rights through succession, contract, or by changing from "temporary tenure" to "full ownership".
Later, in 2001, the current land law was promulgated, which allowed full private ownership of land in Cambodia and established a system of land registration and land titles for citizens.
At the same time, Cambodia has received assistance from the World Bank in preparing a systematic land registration system to issue land titles to citizens. During the land registration period, we faced a major problem, land disputes, which needed to be addressed urgently to the people. What mechanisms does Cambodia have in resolving land disputes?
To find out more about the “Land Dispute Resolution Mechanism in Cambodia” FRESH NEWS Legal Awareness Program in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, please bring this topic up and explain to the following people:
Cadastral Commission Dispute Resolution
The Cadastral Commission was established by sub-decree No. 47 dated 31 May 2002, which includes three (three) levels of cadastral commission: municipal, district, cadastral, cadastral, provincial, and cadastral committees. National level.
The rules on the procedures of the Cadastral Commission are set out in Prakas No. 112 DONS / PRK dated 21 August 2002 on the guidelines and procedures of the Cadastral Commission.
In which the Cadastral Commission has the mission to resolve land disputes (unlicensed land issued by the Cadastral Administration) that is not yet registered in the cadastral area outside the jurisdiction or in the jurisdiction area which cannot be mediated by the administrative committee and is not Land disputes involving claims for inheritance or disputes over contracts for sale, lease or mortgage.
The Cadastral Commission resolves each land dispute, starting from the district level to the provincial capital level and finally to the National Cadastral Commission:
A /. Dispute Resolution by the District Cadastral Commission Receiving Complaints and Responding to Complaints: Every person who has a dispute related to unregistered cadastral land has the right to file a complaint directly to the District Cadastral Commission. The Commission will then notify the parties to the plaintiff or any interested party involved in the land dispute within 30 days of receiving the notice.
Investigation after receiving the complaint: The District Cadastral Commission will send officers to investigate to meet with the parties to the dispute, witnesses, local authorities and to measure the size and boundaries of the disputed land.
After completing the investigation, the Cadastral Commission will check whether the dispute is within its jurisdiction. If the case is not within the jurisdiction, the complaint shall be rejected and the parties to the dispute shall be notified in writing.
If the disputing party is not satisfied with the dismissal, the disputing party may file a complaint with the next higher Cadastral Commission within 30 days after receiving the notice or the dismissal form.
About Administrative Meetings: District and Khan Cadastral Committees shall invite the parties to the dispute to attend the administrative meeting to introduce the process and procedures for resolving disputes.
The parties must select three mediators and be informed of their right to choose a person whom the parties believe can assist them as an assistant. At the same time, the parties shall determine the time, place of settlement with the mediator, or the parties may notify them of any dispute of interest between any party with the Cadastral Commission.
Mediation: After the completion of the investigation, at least 30 days from the date of posting the complaint, the District Cadastral Commission shall convene a meeting to decide on the dispute mediation process and the meeting shall be held at the disputed site or at a place close to the location. That dispute.
Mediation and / or decision of the Cadastral Commission is made without coercion, without intimidation, meaning that it is a settlement based on mediation, i.e. the will of the parties to the dispute who are willing to share their interests. Mutual efforts to find a solution from the mediator. The parties to the dispute and their assistants shall attend the conciliation meeting, and the mediation process shall be subject to the rules of a conciliation.
If the settlement is successful, both parties must thumbprint the dispute resolution form in the agreed content. If the dispute cannot be resolved through mediation, the District Cadastral Commission will refer the dispute to the Capital and Provincial Cadastral Commission for further resolution.
Measurement Preparation: After the dispute has been successfully resolved either through mediation or decision, the District Cadastral Commission will conduct a survey to determine the size of the land and the boundaries as agreed by the parties or by final decision. After that, the Department of Land Management, Urban Planning, Construction and Land, Municipal, District, Khan will start the process of registering plots at the request of the parties to the dispute.
B /. Dispute Resolution by the Capital and Provincial Cadastral Commission After receiving the referral case from the District Cadastral Commission, the Capital and Provincial Cadastral Commission will try to mediate further according to the prescribed procedure. Within 60 days after receiving the dispute, the Capital / Provincial Cadastral Commission shall hold an administrative meeting between the Provincial / Municipal Cadastral Commission and the parties to the dispute.
If the mediation is not successful, the Capital and Provincial Cadastral Committees will have to follow a separate procedure, the decision-making process. If any party does not agree with the decision of the Capital and Provincial Cadastral Commission within 30 working days after receiving the decision or receiving information about the decision, the disputing party has the right to appeal to the National Cadastral Commission for a hearing. Again.
C /. Dispute Resolution by the National Cadastral Commission The National Cadastral Commission has the right to decide any disputes that are outside the jurisdiction and cannot be resolved by the Cadastral Commission at the District, Khan and Cadastral Commission levels of the Capital and Provinces. Occurs in a jurisdiction that cannot be resolved by the Administrative Commission.
In cases where disputes have been referred by the Capital, Provincial or Cadastral Commission, the National Cadastral Commission will try to mediate, but if the mediation is not successful, the National Cadastral Commission will hold a hearing. The parties have the right to appeal the decision of the National Cadastral Commission to the court within 30 days after receiving this decision.
2: Dispute resolution by the judiciary
The court has jurisdiction to resolve disputes over land that has been registered and land that has already been granted a title, according to Joint Proclamation No. 03 dated November 26, 2003 of the Ministry of Justice and the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction.
In summary, there are two types of mechanisms for resolving land disputes in Cambodia: dispute resolution mechanisms by the Cadastral Commission for unregistered land and settlements by the judiciary for land that has already been registered and has a title deed. Therefore, please note that all unregistered land disputes must be referred to the Cadastral Commission before filing a lawsuit.
