(Siem Reap): Archaeological excavations at Banteay Kdei began on the 10th and ended on the 21st of August 2019 under the collaboration between the APSARA Authority and the University of Sofia, Japan and the Royal University of Fine Arts. The evolution of the structure and relationship of each building, as well as the training of students of the Faculty of Archeology and Architecture in the training program of Sofia University.
Nhim Sotheavin, a researcher at Sofia University in Japan, said that in 2019, the team excavated at a site called "Khoeun Buddhism" in order to clarify the 1995 and 1996 excavations. Made in the western part of the Buddhist temple. At that time, the team found some urns made of Chinese pottery made during the 16th and 17th centuries.
Although it is not possible to set a date for the construction of C19, it is understood that it was built before Buddhism, which is based on the foundations of the building excavated by the team. The team will repeat the excavation next year to find out the details of the structure of the two buildings.
According to Rohal villagers, in the 1980s, Rohal and Srah Srang villagers (villages near the temple) buried their urns here, some at Angkor Wat. In the 1970s, when heavy bombings took place, many villagers near Banteay Kdei flocked to the temple grounds for safety. At that time, relatives' coffins were also buried around the Buddhist shrine.
To this day, during the Khmer New Year and before Pchum Ben, villagers often come here to hold ceremonies to commemorate the departed relatives. The team will also continue to search for relatives who buried the urn at this location to know the details after the excavation.
Please note that in the last 30 years we have understood the evolution and timing of the structure of each structure. Although Banteay Kdei was built during the reign of Jayavarman VII in the late 12th century, we still find some structures in the 10th and 11th centuries.
In addition, the team excavated and found many artefacts in the Banteay Kdei area, especially in 2000 and 2001, the team excavated and found 274 Buddha statues, and in 2010 found 6 more pieces. Today, the statues are on display at the Norodom Sihanouk-Angkor Museum. Minister Pan Sosak Asks Commerce Department Officials to Do Four Things to Strengthen Good Governance Practices
(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Commerce, Mr. Pan Sosak, requested officials to do four important tasks to strengthen the implementation of good governance, which is the core of the fourth phase of the Rectangular Strategy.
These include: 1. Efforts to expand knowledge at both professional and foreign language levels; Second, to work closely with local authorities to promote the in-depth reform programs of the Royal Government and the Ministry of Commerce; and 3: Absolutely avoid external fees Official from the private sector and fourth: cooperate with the working group to strengthen the capital-provincial Department of Commerce to solve challenges in their institutions.
The Minister made these proposals during a meeting with leaders and officials of the Department of Commerce of Phnom Penh on Friday morning, August 23, 2019 to monitor the progress of the Department and disseminate the main achievements of the Ministry of Commerce. As well as the work that the Royal Government and the Ministry are continuing to promote the development of national trade.
In addition to the above proposal, Minister Pan Sosak highlighted the efforts of the Royal Government and the Ministry of Commerce to participate in the implementation of the decentralization and deconcentration reform strategy adopted in June 2005 in order to strengthen environmental cooperation. Involvement from all parties) between national and sub-national institutions, which is an important foundation for ensuring social development and diversification of sustainable economic growth.
