(Siem Reap): Inscription experts of the APSARA Authority confirm that more than 1,400 inscriptions of Khmer ancestors (ancient Khmer and Pali Sanskrit) have been researched, found and registered, at least more than 1,000 pages have been translated and some are being translated into modern Khmer.
Ms. Chhom Kunthea, an expert on inscriptions at the International Center for Research and Documentation of Angkor under the APSARA Authority, stated that there is some evidence that the ancients considered inscriptions to be like relics, such as statues. At Bayon temple, there is a ceremony with an inscription placed on a high place and people celebrate and celebrate.
This confirmation was made in the lecture on "Trying to examine Cambodian inscriptions through the eyes of ancient people" at the APSARA Authority on August 16, 2019.
According to Ms. Chhom Kunthea, stone inscriptions or inscriptions are carefully protected. In addition, there is some evidence that the ancient inhabitants wrote inscriptions to teach the general public the "dharma", that is, to do only non-sinful festivals.
Ms. Chhom Kunthea continued that the inscriptions, in short, related to God and the King are all worshipers, there are no inscriptions on other topics such as addition of arithmetic in mathematics.
Indeed, almost all ancient people, not only Cambodians, always respected the letter or the text, because this letter is probably the first technology that man made, so there is no wonder that people respect Letters or writing.
She added that to date, more than 1,400 inscriptions have been researched and registered, including the ancient Khmer and Pali Sanskrit languages. At least 1,000 inscriptions have been translated and some are still being translated.
Ms. Chhom Kunthea explained that, more importantly, according to the experts, the Sanskrit inscriptions written in Cambodia are more famous than the same Sanskrit inscriptions in India, the original country of this language, which is famous. That is clear in both appearance and meaning.
Nong Hep Finance (Cambodia) CC is a subsidiary of Nong Hep Bank of the Republic of Korea. Nong Hep is a bank established in 1961 with 100% Korean capital and is a specialized bank that manages both central and regional government deposits securely with an extensive branch network. Most across the country.
The Bank's reputation not only stands for financial services, but also for its role in promoting social and agricultural welfare in the Republic of Korea. Nong Hep has penetrated overseas markets through overseas bank branches such as the United States, Vietnam and microfinance institutions in Myanmar and Cambodia, and has representative offices in China and India.
Nong Hep Finance received a license from the National Bank of Cambodia on August 08, 2018 to operate as a microfinance institution after Nong Hep Bank acquired a 100% stake in SAMIC Microfinance Institution Plc. Currently, Nong Hep Microfinance Institution operates in Phnom Penh and eight provinces, including Kandal, Kampong Speu, Takeo, Kampot, Kep, Sihanoukville, Siem Reap and Kampong Thom.
In addition to financial services, which is its core business, NongHub Finance has been actively involved in social work to help its clients improve their daily living through the implementation of the Education Credit Credits (CWE) program. Provide training to people in rural areas.
Both client and non-client agricultural techniques, including family animal husbandry techniques, rice production techniques, including training on packaging and food safety techniques, training on household budget management, and Community education on health and hygiene.
In fact, in 2018, Nong Heap Finance provided this training to 4,666 participants, of which 66% women and 22% are Nong Heap Finance customers.
In addition, in July 2019, Nong Heap Finance Microfinance Institution also conducted a training on family animal husbandry techniques for the people in Prey Kuy village, Prey Kuy commune, Kampong Svay district, Kampong Thom province with the participation of A total of 126 people.
