Mr. Kong Monika, President of the Khmer Will Party, expressed his pleasure at the increase in the Cambodian Court of Appeal, which will now act as a regional appeal court, allowing people in the provinces to travel to justice services better than coming to Phnom Penh. Finding justice at the Court of Appeal in Phnom Penh is time consuming and expensive.
"I, in the Supreme Advisory Council, which met with the Minister of Justice of the Khmer Will Party, requested the establishment of this Court of Appeal," he said. "So it is in line with the policy of the Ministry of Justice as well as the Royal Government. Now the positive results that make up this Court of Appeal, so I am very pleased with this."
He thought that Cambodia should have more regional appeals courts. However, the president of this political party expressed the view that our society can not do anything to immediately want.
At the same time, Mr. Pich Sros, President of the Cambodian Youth Party, also praised the establishment of the Court of Appeal in Phnom Penh, Tbong Khmum, Battambang and Sihanoukville. He noted that the establishment was the right process to make it easier for people to go to the local appellate courts to avoid wasting time, and this was to establish legal services to the area and make it easier for people to sue in that area.
According to Mr. Pich Sros, these four Court of Appeals are sufficient for the current context. He said the establishment of the new Court of Appeal was a survey of law enforcement and if the implementation was good, it could be established in other areas.
"So it is to strengthen law enforcement and transparency in the local legal system," Pich Sros said.
Separately, Mr. Sieng Sok, Prosecutor of the Preah Sihanouk Provincial Court, also welcomed the establishment of the Phnom Penh Court of Appeal and three other regional Court of Appeals, expressing optimism that it has many benefits in relation to the process of proceeding faster and faster. . However, prosecutors believe that these four appeals courts are just enough to respond to the current social context.
Mr. Sieng Sok stated: "In my view, it is to support the people who benefit a lot, especially those who have cases to be processed in Phnom Penh, now we divide by region, so as to reduce congestion." ... In my opinion, it is enough because we have the appeal of the area as divided into regions.
Mr. Toch Sopheakdey, Prosecutor of Pailin Provincial Court, also congratulated the King on signing the new Court of Appeal, as cases have always been referred to Phnom Penh. He said that sending an appeal to Phnom Penh was a long way off, and that having defendants in prison made it difficult for the prison.
"It is difficult to send the accused from distant prisons, for example in Pailin province, to clear the prison in Phnom Penh," he said. So the establishment of regional appeals courts, for example, in Battambang, the Battambang prison sent to it takes a short time.
On the social side, civil society is congratulating and appreciating the establishment of more new appeals courts. Son Senkaruna, a senior official at the Adhoc Human Rights Association, said it would make the case less congested and promote human rights.
Mr. Son Senkaruna said that in the past, it was very difficult for people who have stories in the provinces to spend a lot of time and money to come to Phnom Penh. Citizens' cases are concentrated in the Phnom Penh Court of Appeal alone, affecting the rights of citizens, both for the plaintiffs and defendants, as well as other stakeholders.
According to Son Senkaruna, he wants more appeals courts. He wants to see more appeals courts so that it becomes a central point that can make it easier for people to find justice services near them.
It should be noted that His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of Cambodia, has issued a Royal Decree establishing the Phnom Penh Court of Appeal and the Court of Appeal in three more areas: Tbong Khmum, Battambang and Sihanoukville to facilitate the case. Of the people in each province. This is according to the Royal Decree obtained by Fresh News on August 22, 2019.
The four Court of Appeals will soon be divided into areas to receive cases and will take effect on April 1, 2020. This is according to Mr. Sam Pracheamanit, Secretary General of the Supreme Council of the Magistracy, confirmed to Fresh News.
Pursuant to the Royal Decree, the Phnom Penh Court of Appeal, located in Phnom Penh, shall accept appeals against decisions of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court and the Prosecutor of the Phnom Penh and Kandal Provincial Courts or the cases referred by the Supreme Court. While the area of the Court of Appeal in other areas for the settlement of cases of people in the provinces in nearby areas.
