China warns: US companies selling arms to Taiwan will face sanctions from Beijing
"China condemns such a decision by the United States," a Chinese spokesman said. "We have strongly opposed the US side and called on Washington to fully recognize the serious dangers posed by arms sales to Taiwan."
The statement was made in response to the Pentagon's approval of the sale of 66 latest F-16V fighter jets worth more than $ 8 billion to Taiwan.
In July, Beijing warned Washington against interfering in Taiwan's internal affairs and demanded that the United States end all official and military ties between Washington and Taipei, but Washington remained silent. For this warning remains the same.
China formally severed ties with Taiwan in 1949 when the island declared independence from China, while mainland China and Taiwan re-established diplomatic relations in the late 1980s. China, in particular, has repeatedly blamed the United States for trying to provide military aid to the island. What the world and including the United States have officially recognized is that Taiwan is just one of China's provinces.
