18 young people from 8 countries gained a lot of experience during their internship in Angkor
(Siem Reap): Through the Youth Volunteer Program for World Heritage in Angkor, 18 young people from eight countries, including Cambodia, Korea, France, Australia, Colombia, Bulgaria, India and China, gained a wealth of experience while receiving training on conservation management and conservation. Sustainable development in Angkor.
Through this program, they also exchanged a lot of experiences related to the field of heritage as well as community engagement, including Juanita Erasso, a young volunteer from Colombia, said that this is the first time she has participated. As a young volunteer at Angkor Resort and learned about the management, repair, conservation of the community and understand the Angkor Code of Conduct, which is the point that every tourist must follow.
The Colombian said that the Angkor site is a large archeological site and that people here continue to lively practice their religious beliefs. As for heritage management, it is different from Colombia, but in the goal of preserving the same human heritage, and she will share this good experience in her country in the field of heritage.
Mr. Chum Seakhor, a youth volunteer from the Department of Tourism Development of the National Authority of Preah Vihear, said that through this training, the youth group went to explore the various sites in the Angkor resort, and each location is different, such as the repair of brick temples. Stones, irrigation management, conservation of pottery and communities.
He said that the training provided a lot of knowledge, especially about the complex repair methods that he did not know, and those activities further motivate young people to love the ancestral heritage even more.
He added that he would apply that knowledge to his institution and asked for this program to continue to increase the relationship between youth and youth on a global scale.
Mr. Sok Sangvar, Deputy Director General of APSARA Authority, expressed his belief that when people know something well, they will start to love and respect, then they will want to protect it for a long time and share it with others to understand.
That is why this program has been organized so that young people can understand the value of Angkor and the stories behind this greatness. The core idea is not just for Angkor, but for heritage sites around the world that we should all explore.
He also asked the young people to return and share their experiences and knowledge from here to their families, friends and community.
