Greenland is located between the North Atlantic and the Arctic Ocean. The huge island covers an area of 2.17 million square kilometers, with a population of only 56,200, and is an Danish autonomous region that receives two-thirds of the budget revenue of an icy island. This. Greenland is covered by more than 80 percent of the ice, with some areas up to 4 km thick and lasting for days (or no nights) for two months each year.
* Why did Greenland become an autonomous region or belong to Denmark?
Greenland was initially under Norwegian rule, but later the Norwegians living here began to die (perhaps due to the harsh living conditions). In 1380, Denmark began to expand its influence instead by settling near the capital, Nuuk, and by 1500, the Norwegians were gradually leaving.
Then, in 1931, Norway sued Denmark in the International Court of Justice to claim sovereignty over the archipelago, but the court ruled that Denmark was the winner, and it was not until 1953 that the international community recognized Greenland as a province. Of Denmark.
Greenland gained autonomy with its own parliament and government, as well as the right to control natural resources after a 2008 referendum. Currently, US President Donald Trump is reportedly considering buying the island from Denmark, while the US military already has a Thule Air Base on Greenland.
