Seeing the growing demand for recycled wood, plywood, plywood and furniture in Cambodia in 2011, the Royal Government provided 34,000 hectares in Sambo, Kratie and Siem Bok districts of Stung Treng province to the company. Sing BioTech (Cambodia) Co., Ltd. to plant trees for processing into planks and furniture.
Seeing the demand for wood in the wood processing industry, the company has planned to plant all kinds of seedlings on an additional 10 thousand hectares for 10 years from 2019 to 2028.
Mr. Veng Sakhon said that the area that allows the company to plant trees is the land that the Royal Government has set aside for planting trees in 2008. However, due to the limited state budget, it has also partnered with the private sector to work on planting saplings to serve the processing, furniture and construction sectors. Considering the planting of acacia and wind oil trees that can be processed into furniture and used in the construction sector.
In addition to clearing the land and planting seedlings to support tree planting projects to support the wood processing industry, the company has also established a large scientific research and breeding institute in Kampong Thom province on an area of approximately 37 hectares. The institute plans to produce about 10 million seedlings a year.
In addition to the current tree planting activities, the company also has two wood processing plants to process wood chips into planks. One is in Phnom Penh and the other is in Kampong Cham.
Both factories are actively processing wood chips or piles into planks to supply markets in high-demand countries.
Mr. Lu Chu Chhang said that currently Cambodia needs about 20,000 cubic meters of concrete per month. In that, his company can produce to fill the market between 3,500 cubic meters to 5,000 cubic meters, other than to be imported from outside.
According to Lu Chuchang, the demand for concrete boards could increase by about 20 percent a year if the growth of construction in Cambodia continues to increase as it is today.
The Minister of Agriculture, Mr. Veng Sakhon, expressed his support for the company's development project, which planted trees to be processed into furniture, such as plywood. The minister said that each year, tens of thousands of cubic meters of wood, furniture and planks are imported from below to meet domestic demand.
Through this tree planting and processing development project, Sing Bay Otec not only contributes to the national economy and promotes the wood processing industry in Cambodia, but also provides many jobs for Cambodians.
At plantations in Kratie and Stung Treng provinces, nurseries in Kampong Thom, as well as at wood processing factories in Kampong Cham and Phnom Penh, the company provides nearly 2,000 jobs, earning between $ 250 and more than $ 1,000. Dollars. Mr. Veng Sakhon handed over a house and groceries to an 80-year-old woman living in Kampong Siem District, Kampong Cham Province
