Understand the law: the payment of stamp duty on the transfer of ownership or possession of real estate
(Phnom Penh): Currently, the real estate sector in Cambodia is very dynamic, both people and investors have invested in many real estate sectors, and through the growth of this sector, the state also receives a lot of revenue. By collecting stamp duty. Stamp tax is a tax levied by the state through the transfer of ownership or possession of real estate, for example.
For this week, Fresh News' Legal Awareness Program, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, would like to raise the topic of stamp duty on transfer of ownership or ownership of real estate to distribute to citizens as well as students. Below:
For immovable property registered with the title deed (hard title deed), when the immovable property is transferred, the recipient is obliged to pay the stamp duty on the transfer at the rate of 4% (four percent) in accordance with the law in force.
In order to pay the stamp duty on the transfer of ownership, when applying for the transfer of ownership or tenure at the Cadastral Administration, the owner and the transferee must complete the application for stamp duty in the form issued by the General Department of Taxation. With the ownership transfer file, the property location diagram and the identification notes that specify the actual size of the property issued by the Cadastral Administration.
After completing the stamp duty application form in the form of the General Department of Taxation, the owner or the successor has the right to send the documents to the tax administration for actual evaluation and determine the amount of tax to be paid into the state budget. .
For unregistered immovable property (soft title), when the immovable property is transferred, the transferee is obliged to pay stamp duty at the rate of 4% (four%) in accordance with the law in force same as the transfer. Ownership of real estate with a solid title deed as well.
It should also be noted that unregistered properties that have been transferred at the commune, sangkat, or municipal, district, and khan levels with multiple transfer histories before January 1, 2017, are allowed to pay stamp duty only once. Finally, with the preference to remove the stamp duty base of 600,000,000 (six hundred million) Riels only for the property that was initially registered with the Cadastral Administration.
The granting of the exemption from the above stamp duty is valid only for the property that has applied for initial registration with the Cadastral Administration by December 31, 2018.
Separately, for unregistered real estate that has been transferred several times before January 1, 2017, one last stamp duty must be paid without allowing the deduction of the tax base of 600,000,000 ( Six hundred million) Riel as real estate requested for registration.
The basis of this stamp duty is determined in the following order of priority:
1. Property prices determined in the appendix to Prakas No. 343 MEF.PrK dated 22 March 2019 of the Ministry of Economy and Finance. 2. The value of the immovable property specified in the transfer of ownership or right of possession or other relevant legal documents in the event that the value is equal to or higher than the value as stated in Annexure to Prakas 343 MEF.Prk dated 22 March 2012. 2019 of the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
In addition, the Royal Government has eased many burdens on the people to pay the stamp duty related to the transfer of ownership or transfer of ownership within the relatives and other activities where the conditions of the stamp duty exemption are Set as follows:
At the same time, the Royal Government also favors taxes on the transfer of ownership or possession of real estate within the family, made between siblings and between parents-in-law and son-in-law is allowed to be deducted from the tax base to stamp the amount. 200,000,000 (two hundred million) Riels for succession and 100,000,000 (one hundred million) Riels for donations.
Separately, the transfer of ownership or possession of the same property from the second time onwards as a gift within the relatives between the birth parents and the children between the husband and wife, between the grandparents and the grandchildren is allowed to deduct the tax base. Stamp of 100,000,000 (one hundred million) Riels.
Tax exemption or stamp duty in the above relatives must be documented as proof of relative relations such as family book, birth certificate, marriage certificate and / or other documents that can prove the relationship.
The transfer of ownership or possession of exempted property or tax incentives equal to 0 (zero) Riels shall also follow the procedures and procedures at the tax administration in force to register and record the data of all transfers of ownership. Or the right to occupy those properties.
In summary, stamp duty is an important part of the state revenue collected from the transfer of ownership or possession of real estate and activities as defined in the Prakas of the Ministry of Economy and Finance for the benefit of the nation.
