Very symphonic! Husband and wife die due to fishing tackle, leaving 1-year-old daughter orphaned in Preah Vihear province
(Preah Vihear): A horrific case of a market and a tragedy occurred in a poor family, but the monarchy killed the couple because of the fishing tackle that they use to catch fish to sell for money. The family died embracing the two, leaving their one-year-old daughter to live alone and helpless.
This incident happened on the evening of August 16, 2019 at the point of Dangkep Kdam canal east of Bangkorn village, Reak Reay commune, Rovieng district, Preah Vihear province.
According to Colonel Say Det, Police Inspector of Rovieng District, confirmed to Fresh News this morning, August 17, 2019, that before the incident, the two victims, a couple, went to the scene. Up to catch fish, but accidentally shocked himself, causing the death of both.
The district inspector confirmed that the authorities concluded that the two victims, a couple, died from the fish shock device because on the back of the victim, the husband was attached to the fish shock battery, while the wife's body was hugged from above because Husband fell and jumped to help, causing the shock to kill both of them at once.
Colonel Say Det added that the bodies of the victims were 25-year-old Dith Pon and 23-year-old Long Phen, a resident of Bangkorn village, Reak Reay commune, Rovieng district, Preah Vihear province. One-year-old corpse was taken to the family for a traditional ceremony at home.
CamControl officers found 61 kilograms of expired goods for sale at Kang Keng Market, Preah Sihanouk Province and confiscated and burned
(Preah Sihanouk): For food safety as well as the health of the people, on August 16, 2019, Camcontrol Preah Sihanouk Branch, with Mr. Bun Chamroeun, Acting Branch Manager, with the participation of the Provincial Department of Commerce, Market Commission and local authorities continued. Disseminate the quality of food safety, inspect food products and pre-packaged goods at Kang Keng Market, Sihanoukville, Preah Sihanouk Province, finding 61 kg of expired goods.
During the inspection, the officers took some food samples for preliminary test, including fresh shrimp, squid, navy beans and noodles to find banned chemicals that can affect people's health.
As a result, officials did not find the presence of banned chemicals in the seafood, but found the display of defective and expired goods, including:
After that, the professional officer made a record of confiscating the goods and storing them at the Sihanoukville branch for destruction, and instructed other traders to understand and follow the law on quality control, safety of products and services to avoid causing harm. Impact on people's health will be able to face the law further.
