Student Judges, Internal Judges and Clerks of the Royal Academy of Judicial Professions visit the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia
The study tour is designed to give them a first-hand look at the Khmer Rouge tribunal, the United Nations Joint Tribunal for Cambodia, which has jurisdiction over former senior leaders and senior officials. The most heinous crimes committed were committed during the Democratic Kampuchea regime.
The visit of the students, judges and clerks also aims to learn about the progress of the Khmer Rouge tribunal's mission, the progress of the case and the administration of the ECCC. As well as the opportunity to see the ECCC courtroom. Along with an explanation of the actual hearing process.
Several speakers who are officials of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia presented their experiences.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Director of Public Affairs, outlined the history of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia, as well as the proceedings before the Chamber, from its inception in early February 2006 to 2019.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra also emphasized the importance of establishing the Khmer Rouge Tribunal to seek justice for the victims of the Khmer Rouge regime, to participate in the search for truth, national reconciliation and national reconciliation, and to strengthen peace in Cambodia, to reconcile the suffering victims. Caused by the Khmer Rouge and the memory to prevent and prevent the return of the brutal regime of the Khmer Rouge regime.
Mr. Thong Socheat, Officer-in-Charge of the Office of Administration of the ECCC, outlined the working structure of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia, as well as the working system of the Khmer Tribunal.
Mr. Thong Socheat also mentioned the archives and detention of the accused.
