Mr. Hun Many handed over a letter of thanks to the troops who participated in the rescue of the building collapse in Sihanoukville
(Phnom Penh): The 70th Brigade on the morning of August 13, 2019, held a ceremony to thank the forces that participated in the rescue operation in the building collapse in Sihanoukville last June.
The ceremony was presided over by Mr. HUN Many, Member of the National Assembly, Chairman of the Seventh Committee of the National Assembly and President of the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia, and Lt. Gen. Mao Sophan, Deputy Commander of the Army, Commander of the 70th Brigade, accompanied by Minister Say Sam Al. The Ministry of Environment and the chairman of the national working group to help Sihanoukville and many other stakeholders.
Speaking on the occasion, Lt. Gen. Mao Sophan confirmed that today, the 711 Emergency Squadron has saved the lives of people for first aid, to save lives and to alleviate the situation for the victims. Fires and accidents along National Roads 3, 4 and the western districts of Phnom Penh and send victims to government hospitals.
In particular, recently, the 711 Emergency Corps participated in the search and rescue operation for the victims of the building collapse in Sihanoukville and went down to rescue a man who fell near a rock for 3 nights 4 days in the cave The Phnom Hong Teuk rock in Battambang province for the Emergency and Fire Brigade 711 has helped to alleviate the difficulties and rescue the affected people.
At the same time, Mr. HUN Many said that as a Cambodian citizen, he reminded the heroic example of all the forces and rescue teams who sacrificed their physical and mental strength to save the lives of the victims. Caring for our Cambodian family in difficult times and in all circumstances.
Please be informed that on June 22, 2019, a 7-storey building collapsed in Sihanoukville, killing 28 people and injuring 26 others. The mission lasted for 3 nights and 4 days. A total of 1,487 people were rescued. Among them, the 70th Infantry Brigade sent 71 troops and other rescue equipment on time and received the highest attention.
