Rainstorms have hit the highlands of central Vietnam, causing landslides and floods since August 2. In the south, about 3,900 homes were damaged, more than 30 kilometers of roads were damaged and more than 22,000 hectares of farmland were flooded.
According to Vietnamese authorities, floods, storms and landslides have killed 181 people and left 37 missing and caused $ 858 million in damage in 2018 in the Southeast Asian country. .
APSARA Authority takes action to stop mats, hammocks and equipment around the odd pond in front of the Brahma Kel temple and on the grass around the Angkor site
(Siem Reap): The APSARA Authority has announced to the people and tourists to stop mats, hammocks and other materials around the odd pond in front of the Brahma Kel temple and on the grass around the Angkor Wat temple.
According to the Apsara Authority, some traders and tourists are now laying mats, hammocks and other materials around the odd pond in front of the Brahma Kel temple and on the grass around the Angkor Wat temple to eat and relax.
These activities have affected the value of cultural heritage as well as the environment and public order of Angkor.
In the past, the APSARA Authority has cooperated with the Heritage Protection Police Unit and local authorities to stop these activities, but still some business people and tourists refuse to stop these activities for personal gain.
In this regard, in order to promote and universally value the value of Angkor resort as well as national honor, APSARA Authority would like to ask all traders and tourists to cooperate in stopping the activities of mats, hammocks and other materials around the odd pond in front of Brahma Kel temple and On the grass around Angkor Wat, leave for 45 days (forty-five days) from the date of this notice.
In the event that traders and tourists continue these activities, the committee leading and coordinating the order of vendors in the Angkor resort will implement administrative measures to confiscate those materials without any responsibility for any damage. Tomorrow, Cambodia will host the 3rd National Interfaith Forum "To Combat Human Trafficking and Modern Slavery"
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia will organize the 3rd National Interfaith Forum "To Combat Human Trafficking and Modern Slavery" on August 14, 2019 at the Koh Pich Exhibition and Convention Center under the chairmanship of Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister. Interior and Chairman of the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking (NEC).
The National Interfaith Forum is organized by the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking, the Ministry of Cults and Religions, and Caritas Cambodia, with the support of religious leaders, including Buddhists, Muslims, Catholics and Evangelicals, and NGOs. Work to combat human trafficking in Cambodia.
The three main objectives of this forum are:
* First, to promote the importance of the interfaith forum in Cambodia, which reflects the harmony of religion in Cambodia, brings national peace, respect for human rights, dignity and the progress of humanity.
* Second: To show the important role of religious leaders in educating and educating people about the morality of life, avoiding abuse, trafficking, exploitation of all forms of people and help to prevent Prevent and fight to eradicate human trafficking, a disgusting modern slavery.
* Third: Many activities were organized on that occasion, such as the procession and distribution of messages containing educational and anti-trafficking messages for public dissemination, exhibitions, activities to participate in the fight against human trafficking, speeches and recommendations of Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Interior, Chairman of the NEC, prayed and posted a joint message.
