Ms. Lum Kuntheary visited the lives of the three elderly families in their daily lives, and she always took into account the well-being of the general homeless, especially the elderly or the vulnerable. Regardless of political affiliation.
Please note that the gifts distributed to the three elderly families include: 25 kg of rice, some food items and sarongs, fish, soy sauce, pure water, fish sauce, sarong, noodles, drinks and some money received from the Women's Association and the Women's Movement, and some more. Received from Mr. Oknha Heng Samnang, President of Kampong Thom-Preah Vihear Chamber of Commerce.
Deputies and Governor of Kratie Province Organize Safe Pork Eating Ceremony at Samaki Market, Kratie City After African Swine Outbreak
(Kratie): The Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries of Kratie Province on August 9, 2019 organized a safe pork eating ceremony in front of Kratie Samaki Market under the chairmanship of Mr. Chea Vandeth, Chairman of the Third Committee of the National Assembly and Mr. Vathon, Provincial Governor. Kratie, as well as the participation of government officials around the province, the three forces, together with a large number of people and traders.
Kratie provincial governor urges Kratie residents to return to normal eating meat without fear of contracting African swine fever because the disease is not contagious This African pig is not infected and does not affect our human health.
Mr. Chea Vandeth confirmed that after Samdech Techo Hun Sen announced that people continue to eat pork, all provincial authorities and provincial agriculture departments, veterinary officials, along with some other officials, ate pork in a safe pork eating program.
In order to inspire and show to all people, do not worry about the African swine fever, because according to the regular monitoring of the Ministry of Cambodia so far is not transmitted to humans, it only infects pigs. Only.
According to officials from the Veterinary Office, in recent weeks, traders who raise pigs and sell pork have been complaining that pork sales have been steadily declining. An outbreak of African swine fever has caused people to suspend or reduce their consumption of pork.
