Without INF treaty, US Department of Defense can order new missile launchers
The agreement comes less than a week after the United States pulled out of the Intermediate-Range Nuclear Missile (INF) treaty on Friday. This is seen as the killing of an agreement that has provided stability and peace to Europe for the past 32 years.
For Russia and China, Washington's decision to withdraw from the INF is to allow the government to develop a new generation of medium-range mobile nuclear weapons and conventional missiles to replace Pershing II. Abandoned under the above treaty. In particular, such weapons could be deployed to threaten Russia and China.
As soon as the United States withdrew from the INF, Russia announced its complete withdrawal and threatened to develop nuclear weapons. According to previous analysts, the US withdrawal is not just to confront Russia, but China is another big issue for the United States, especially in maintaining Washington's influence in Asia.
