After withdrawing from the INF Treaty, Russian President Vladimir Putin warned that Moscow would start developing medium-range nuclear warheads if Washington dared to develop such weapons first. But in fact, the US military has already announced plans to test-fire ballistic missiles banned under the former INF treaty, scheduled to take place between August and November 2019.
The U.S. military has also confirmed that 1,000-kilometer ballistic missiles could be deployed within 18 months of testing, and 3,000-pound ballistic missiles could be deployed. 4,000 km may take 5 years or more to deploy.
So everyone is just waiting to see the Russian side's decision, while many experts warn that the world is on the brink of a Cold War and a renewed arms race. But if the United States and Russia increase the development of nuclear missiles, other countries such as China, North Korea, Pakistan, India and Iran will not stand still and try to find ways to increase their capabilities.
US Secretary of Defense Mark Esper did not specify the exact location or intent of the missile deployment plan in Asia, saying only that it was based on discussions with US allies. But Australia and South Korea were quick to deny that they would accept US missiles deployed on their territory.
