Khmer Rouge tribunal officially announces Nuon Chea death
A statement from the Khmer Rouge tribunal states that the Khmer-Soviet Friendship Hospital informed the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) that Nuon Chea had died at 6 p.m. On the evening of August 4, 2019 at the hospital. Nuon Chea's family was informed of his death.
Khmer Rouge tribunal announces Nuon Chea death announcement tonight
(Phnom Penh): On the night of August 4, 2019, the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia will hold a conference at the Khmer-Soviet Union Hospital to announce the death of Nuon Chea, a former senior leader of the Khmer Rouge regime. This is according to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, spokesman for the Khmer Rouge tribunal.
Nuon Chea, the second eldest brother of the Khmer Rouge regime, died at 6:00 pm on August 4, 2019 at the Khmer-Soviet Union Hospital.
Mr. Pheaktra said that a press conference on Nuon Chea's death would be organized by the Public Affairs Section of the Khmer Rouge Tribunal, with the participation of the Co-Prosecutors and the Defense of Nuon Chea at the Khmer-Soviet Union Hospital. Who is Nuon Chea?
(Phnom Penh): Nuon Chea, the former leader of the Khmer Rouge regime, passed away on the evening of August 4, 2019 at the age of 93 in a Russian hospital. He is known as the second eldest brother of Khmer Rouge cadres after Pol Pot.
Who is Nuon Chea? The following is a detailed biography of Nuon Chea presented by DC-Cam:
Nuon Chea, originally named Lao Kim Ron, later changed to Lao Di, popularly known as Nuon Chea, but his nickname, Khnouch, was his mother's favorite. Nuon Chea was born on July 7, 1926 in Watkor village, Watkor commune, Sangke district, Battambang province. Nuon Chea is the third of nine children.
The Trial Chamber of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) held its first hearing in June 2011 and Case 002 was soon split into two separate cases, Case 002/01 and 002/02. . The dismissal of Case 002 was due to the size and complexity of the Closing Order and the age of the accused.
Although Nuon Chea was a criminal, he was taken care of by a doctor until he died at a Russian hospital on the evening of August 4, 2019.
The following are stories related to Nuon Chea's life, including education, work, family, and involvement in crime during the Khmer Rouge regime:
The story of Nuon Chea and his family, Nuon Chea's father, Lao Liv, and mother, Dos Panh. His father was a corn trader and other businessman. His mother, on the other hand, was a tailor and helped his father with his work. Nuon Chea's family was prosperous, but his family was heavily indebted after the economic downturn after World War I.
Nuon Chea is married to Ly Kimseng, who was born in Kampong Chhnang province and is of Chinese descent. He has four children, three of whom are adopted and one is adopted.
* Nuon Chea's educational background:
During World War II, some provinces in Cambodia were controlled by Thailand. Among them, Battambang is also under Thai control. People in Battambang province at that time were automatically granted Thai citizenship by the Thai government. Nuon Chea also obtained Thai citizenship.
During that time Nuon Chea was only about 15 years old and he was not very interested in what was happening in Cambodia. After Nuon Chea graduated from high school in Battambang province, he left for Bangkok, Thailand to live and continue his studies.
Upon arrival in Thailand, Nuon Chea lived with monks at the Benjamabopit pagoda, who also traveled from Battambang like Nuon Chea. Nuon Chea lived under the care of the three monks and then enrolled in a Thai government-run secondary school called Wat Benjamabopit, on the grounds of the pagoda where he lived. During that time.
Nuon Chea soon enrolled in the seventh grade for a preparatory course at Thammasat University and graduated in 1946 with excellent results. On May 1, 1946, Nuon Chea enrolled in law school at Thammasat University. Nuon Chea's studies in law did not yield good results because he often failed exams in some subjects.
The reason for the decline in his education may be that he did not have much time for study because he was involved in politics and work in Thailand during his studies. Nuon Chea eventually quit both his job in Thailand and his studies at Thammasat University and returned to politics in Cambodia during the 1950s.
* Nuon Chea's work history:
Before 1975, Nuon Chea began to be actively involved in politics since 1940, when he moved to Thailand. During his stay there, Nuon Chea joined the Thai Youth Organization, an organization under the Communist Party and a place where young men and women gather to socialize, help each other, and make changes. Opinions.
Ruam Vong Phan is a former student of Thammasat University and a former roommate of Nuon Chea, who invited Nuon Chea to join the Thai Youth Organization until he became a member of the Thai Communist Party. In addition to this, Nuon Chea also worked as a regular Thai government official, working as a clerk in the accounting office in the General Clearing Department of the Thai Ministry of Finance in late 1947.
After engaging in politics in Thailand, Nuon Chea returned to Cambodia to join the Communist Party's resistance movement, taking charge of the party's propaganda in 1950. In 1959, Tou Samuth, Nuon Chea, and Pol Pot began the process of forming a new communist party, the principles of which were centralized democracy and Marxism-Leninism.
While participating in the Communist Party's resistance movement, Nuon Chea actively interacted with the Vietnamese communists and the Khmer Issarak movement. Nuon Chea also attended political courses in southern and northern Vietnam. Despite the formation of the Kampuchean People's Revolutionary Party, Nuon Chea remained a member of the communist movement.
In September 1960, Nuon Chea was elected Deputy Secretary of the Cambodian People's Revolutionary Party, later renamed the Cambodian Workers' Party and later renamed the Communist Party of Kampuchea, along with Saloth Sar and Tou Samuth. But after Tou Samuth's disappearance in 1962, Nuon Chea remained the party's deputy secretary, during which time Pol Pot became party secretary and was involved in politics until his victory over Lon Nol and his rise to power in 1975.
After the coup d'état of Norodom Sihanouk in 1970, Nuon Chea left Phnom Penh to join the Prey Magi movement with the leaders of the Communist Party of Kampuchea, such as Pol Pot Son, Sentamok and So Phim, setting up their headquarters at Some provinces such as Kampong Thom, Kampong Cham and Kampong Chhnang.
Between 1970 and 1975, Nuon Chea became a senior military commander of the People's Army of the Cambodian National Liberation Movement and the army's political chief, and was also in charge of relations with Vietnam.
During April 1975, Nuon Chea was with Pol Pot and some other important military and political leaders of the Communist Party of Kampuchea, such as Koy Thuon So, Phim Ta Mok, and Son Sen, to lead the forces to occupy Phnom Penh.
By: Un Sodavy, Author of Fact Finding Magazine People of Prek Tonleab Commune Celebrate the Development Project of the Agricultural Land Authority on 400 Hectares
(Kandal): Residents in Prek Tonleab commune, Leuk Dek district, applauded the authorities for developing the agricultural sector on an area of about 400 hectares.
Residents said that in recent years, the cultivation of rice and other vegetables has been more productive as the authorities have dug and rehabilitated the main and child canals and set up irrigation systems to bring water to their fields. With no shortage of water to irrigate crops like before. Residents said that in the past, farming was sometimes not possible, because some years there was a drought and there was no water source for irrigation.
The villagers also claim that only the farmers can grow dry rice twice a year, with an average yield of 6 to 7 tons per hectare. The thing that makes them happier when they harvest is not as difficult to transport as it used to be.
In the past, when it was harvested, it was still difficult to transport because there was no easy road to travel like now, it was a cart road when it rained. Sometimes the truck overturned. At the same time, the people asked the authorities to develop more things, such as paving red stones on the road system to make it easier for them to transport agricultural products.
He added that in addition to the irrigation project, there is also the project of ASD, a French development partner project through the Royal Government, which has two ministries, departments and offices of water resources in the commune, and in this commune there is a pumping station. Three, including two French and one philanthropist.
"In implementing this policy on agriculture, we see that for next year, irrigation water sources will no longer be in short supply. In this, we see that his people are involved in the cultivation of crops, especially rice and other crops such as corn and vegetables. At the same time, we have established a vegetable growing community in the commune that uses organic fertilizers. Mr. Ngy Sary confirmed this.
