Doctors warn of 3 diseases that can affect life if not treated in time
(Siem Reap): Dr. Kham Sophea, a specialist in liver and digestive system at the Royal Phnom Penh-Angkor International Hospital, said that three types of diseases that can cause death include: stomach cancer, biliary obstruction. And pancreatitis.
Dr. Kham Sophea made this remark during a seminar on "Preventing and finding stomach cancer, biliary obstruction, pancreatic disease to people so that they can better understand how to prevent To cause these diseases and effective treatment methods "organized on August 3, 2019.
"These three diseases are serious diseases, if not diagnosed in time and the wrong method can affect life," he said.
He added that the factors that affect the health of people from this disease because they lack awareness, that is why Royal Angkor International Hospital always organizes programs to educate the public about various diseases.
The doctor added that the disease is more common in Cambodia or Thailand, due to the lack of awareness about the prevention of this disease.
The prevention of this disease is to come to the hospital regularly to check health, avoid or stop smoking, alcohol or eating foods that cause a lot of fat, stop taking drugs and eat regularly.
Mr. Narin Tara, a Thai doctor, director of the Royal Phnom Penh and Angkor International Hospital, said that the Royal Phnom Penh and Royal Angkor Hospital is a well-known and trusted hospital for the people of Cambodia and came to share knowledge on prevention. Gastrointestinal cancer, etc. to the people as well as patients to understand this important issue.
He said that patients can consult directly with doctors and treatment at the hospital because there are modern medical equipment that is reliable to the people of Cambodia.
Mr. Hem Chandara, one of the many people who attended the workshop, said that they were satisfied with the Royal Angkor Hospital that provided them with knowledge. He added that he wants other public and private hospitals to provide protection against various diseases, such as the Royal Angkor International Hospital.
