In addition to studying theories in the Angkor Heritage Education Program, children also learn to paint and read more books.
(Siem Reap): The Heritage Education Working Group in collaboration with APSARA Authority and Heritage Protection Organization on the morning of August 1, 2019 took the children out of school to teach more about painting and reading books related to cultural and heritage education. .
The tutoring is to integrate knowledge beyond classrooms or on-site studies and to understand the realities of the challenges they face, as well as impart this knowledge to other children and their communities.
Although in the future, children will be the heirs and guardians of these cultural assets in their communities in the future.
This program is implemented after providing basic knowledge about heritage, which studied both theory in class and visited the resort directly to 165 children from 5th and 6th grade from Srah Srang Primary School and Lalay Primary School for more than 7 years. Months ago.
Learning to paint in order to create a game for primary school children, provide children with knowledge and talent for painting, educate children to know the cultural heritage better in each picture, increase the spirit of children to love and help preserve the heritage more.
On the other hand, reading books is for the purpose of educating about the benefits of joint preservation, protection and joint prevention of vandalism, excavation and antiquities trafficking, inspiring the general public, students and the people living there. Near ancient sites that do not yet understand the value of Khmer cultural heritage, which can cause many ancient sites to face serious threats.
It should be noted that both the content, images and words encourage everyone to better understand the importance of preserving the heritage of the ancestors, as well as encourage the community of these people can be eyes, ears, nose, help monitor, help protect and preserve. Help promote the preservation of these cultural assets to survive for the benefit of the nation in the future.
Senior Minister Kun Kim Meets Officials and Armed Forces in Kandal Province
(Kandal): Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, met with Mr. Mao Phirun, Governor of Kandal Provincial Board of Governors, Provincial Councilors, Deputy Governors and officials on August 2. From the relevant departments, the Armed Forces and development partners in Kandal province, a total of 425 people to discuss and put out some measures to solve the problem caused by the disaster.
During the discussion, Dr. Mao Phirun highlighted the disasters that have occurred in the province in the past. The disasters include: 20 strong winds, damaged 110 houses, drought occurred in 5 districts, affected 2,564 hectares, landslides in 3 rivers, 7 cities / districts, total length 1 230 meters, African swine fever spread in 3 districts, 1,424 pigs died, etc.
According to the provincial governor, in response to these problems, the national team visited Kandal province with the support of the National Committee for Disaster Management, philanthropists, and in cooperation with the provincial branch of the Red Cross and the Cambodian Red Cross. Visit and distribute gifts to the victims' families in a timely manner.
During the meeting, the Senior Minister conveyed the greetings and memories of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen to officials and the Armed Forces throughout Kandal Province.
Senior Minister Kun Kim expressed his appreciation and appreciation for the participation of civil servants, the Armed Forces and the people in the whole province who came together to respond in a timely manner in the event of a disaster and preparedness plan. Better reserves should be proud.
The Senior Minister also emphasized that Kandal Province has achieved many achievements under the leadership of Samdech Techo Prime Minister and has greatly contributed to the new face of Kandal Province with security, safety, beauty and accelerating the development. Both reduce the poverty of the people faster.
(Phnom Penh): The General Commission of the National Police has launched a campaign to promote helmet wearing nationwide for a full month, and on August 2, 2019, the traffic police force in 23 capitals and provinces inspected a number of motorcyclists. Of the 11,232, 4,841, or 43 percent, were forced to wear helmets. This is according to the report of the Traffic Police and Public Order Department, which was obtained by Fresh News.
