Health officials call on people to give their children extra food according to their age to avoid health hazards
(Phnom Penh): In order to contribute to the provision of complete and safe nutrition to infants and young children through the protection and promotion of breastfeeding, the Ministry of Health has organized an executive working group meeting for implementation. Sub-Decree No. 133 on the promotion of products for raising infants and children.
The program was held under the chairmanship of Prof. Am Samol, Undersecretary of State, Ministry of Health and Chair of the Executive Working Group for the Implementation of Sub-Decree No. 133 on August 2, 2019.
"The work we are doing is to encourage the proper and timely feeding of infants and children to ensure the proper use of breast milk substitutes," she said. Only when necessary and with adequate information base.
In addition, parents need to understand that from the time the baby is born until the age of 6 months is to feed only breast milk that has enough nutrients to nourish the body and growth of the baby with a strong immune system or antibodies. It fights against various diseases, especially it is not easy to get and infect any disease from those around you.
She said that from 6 months, parents must provide additional food such as porridge, milk, vegetables, meat, fruits, especially porridge. Because as children grow older, they need more nutrients, and breast milk alone is not enough. However, it is strictly forbidden to give these foods from the time the baby is zero to 6 months old, only the mother's milk is needed.
Please be informed that the meeting was attended by representatives of the Ministry of Information, the Ministry of Industry and Handicrafts, the Ministry of Commerce and representatives of the Department of Medicine, Food, Medical Equipment and Cosmetics, representatives of the National Nutrition Program, representatives of relevant departments under the Ministry of Health, representatives of the World Health Organization. Helen Keller International and UNICEF.
