The draft law on social security aims to establish a Cambodian social security system based on the principles of solidarity and social equity, which includes four sections: pensions, health care, occupational risk and unemployment for individuals in the public sector. Under the Labor Law, including airmen, seafarers and domestic workers, as well as self-employed individuals.
The draft law on the social security system was prepared in accordance with the format introduced by the Office of the Council of Ministers and was prepared by a team of lawyers from the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training in collaboration with the Legal Council of the Council of Ministers and studied. Consult with national legal experts and with development partners, especially employers and unions.
The draft law was submitted to eight inter-ministerial technical meetings on June 28-29, 2018. Chapters on probation were discussed with the Ministry of Justice.
Mr. Phay Siphan said that the draft law is very important for individuals in the public sector, individuals in the private sector and self-employed to receive social security, pensions, health care, occupational risk and unemployment. Work contributes to the improvement of life, dignity and social harmony.
This draft law is also an important legal standard for the implementation of the political program of the Royal Government, the Rectangular Strategy Phase 4 and the implementation of the political program of the Cambodian People's Party, which was announced in the election campaign and has the full support of Voters.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen: The door is still open for political parties to join the Supreme Advisory Council (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): In the meeting of the Council of Ministers this morning, August 2, 2019, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister Of Cambodia stated that only the Supreme Advisory Council mechanism is valid in summoning state institutions outside the National Assembly for questioning.
Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Royal Government Spokesperson Unit, said that during the meeting, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also confirmed that the Supreme Advisory Council is still open to other parties who want to Join as a member.
It should be noted that the Supreme Advisory Council was established by Royal Decree dated September 6, 2018 at the request of Samdech Techo Hun Sen after the 6th National Election. The Supreme Advisory Council is composed of 16 of the 20 political parties that participated in the election.
After its establishment, the Supreme Advisory Council acted in accordance with its duties and responsibilities, including reviewing and advising on draft laws before submitting them to the Royal Government for review and decision. The Supreme Advisory Council also summoned the leaders of various ministries and institutions to inquire about some irregularities under the jurisdiction of those ministries and institutions.
In addition, members of the Supreme Advisory Council are also seen as actively working to resolve issues for the people, especially land disputes in the provinces.
