(Siem Reap): The pottery sector in Cambodia has started to grow and there is a lot of research after the experts of APSARA Authority and national and international institutions have found more than 10 kilns in Angkor since 1995 and researched the type of pottery. Pottery made in kilns and used in the Angkor area, but the Khmer have not yet compiled a technical term related to the naming of pottery and kilns are not yet official.
Therefore, from July 29-31, 2019, the International Center for Research and Documentation of Angkor invited national institutions from the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, Royal Academy, Royal University of Fine Arts, National Museum of Phnom Penh, National Authority of Preah Vihear, Department of Culture and Fine Arts, Koh Kong Province, Banteay Meanchey and Battambang. To organize a school study on "the introduction of technical terms related to pottery."
"It is very useful to call Khmer pottery exactly the technical term, not to mispronounce and call it vaguely, in order to raise the value of Khmer pottery in Southeast Asia as Khmer," he said. "Know how to make hard pottery from the Angkorian period before other countries in Southeast Asia, but they still use the correct technical term."
Mr. Ear Darith continued, "If we do not specify the name of the object, we do not know how to call it to match the functions such as drums for cooking liquids and pots.
The name refers to the function of the current type of pottery and compares it to pottery we find in the past.
On the other hand, sometimes in Angkor it is called like this, but other areas are called differently, so this workshop also focuses on naming from different areas to find more names and Appropriate use according to the cultural context of each region.
Please note that this three-day workshop will teach about Khmer pottery research, discuss technical terms of Khmer pottery in both Khmer and English, inventory registration, artifacts, photography, computer data entry, repair and maintenance. Preserve artifacts for the benefit of everyday workers and as an important document for future research.
Ministry of Health celebrates Dengue Fever Day under the theme "End Dengue Fever Starting From Me"
(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Health, Prof. Mam Bun Heng, this morning, July 31, 2019, led officials, working groups and stakeholders to celebrate Dengue Fever Day under the theme "End Dengue Fever Starting From Me" Held at Sen Sok Primary School, Sangkat Khmuonh, Khan Sen Sok, Phnom Penh.
Minister Mam Bunheng said, "This dengue day is a reminder to all people to take the necessary measures to prevent and fight against dengue fever that is happening in the country. We.
He said that dengue fever is still a public health problem in Cambodia, but the number of cases is declining from 4,769 cases in the second week of July to 3,933 cases in the third week of July 2019.
Minister Mam Bunheng stated that "almost all provinces have seen a decrease in dengue fever, with the exception of Kratie, which has seen a slight increase, and five other provinces, including Phnom Penh, Preah Vihear, Kandal, Kampong Cham and Tbong Khmum, which have seen a slight decrease. "
In response to this situation, the National Dengue Control Program of the National Center for Malaria Control, Parasitology and Anthropology has partnered with relevant units, development partners, local authorities and the media to intervene as follows:
First, the participation movement through the dissemination of health education messages Since the beginning of 2009, these health education through the media have been carried out in parallel with community education through public health at all levels. The celebration of the National Day for the Eradication of Dengue Fever, sponsored today, is a clear symbol of active participation.
Second, provide adequate medicine, serum equipment in the distribution network of the pharmacy and prepare blood for use through the blood supply network. In 2019, the National Program distributed drugs, plasma serum equipment to the provincial level, including more than 200 tons of Abet and planned to provide up to nearly 300 tons from January to July 24, 2019 Banks (Abet and BTI) and mosquito repellents include:
More than 30,000 bottles of serum were distributed to health departments in 25 provinces and cities, and more than 70,000 bottles are planned to be provided. The national program has also prepared more than 6,000 liters of mosquito repellent to combat the ongoing dengue epidemic in Cambodia, and more than 400 liters have been distributed to health departments in 25 provinces.
Third, trained more than 300 dengue trainers and trained more than 1,200 doctors in both public and private services, with the active participation of clinical sub-committees and hospital departments.
Fourth, trained all sub-national national programs to fight dengue fever on technical standards for responding in the event of a large-scale outbreak and on the continuation of regular community outreach.
