This statement was made by Professor Mam Bunheng, Minister of Health, during the celebration of World Breastfeeding Week 2019 on July 30, 2019 at the riverside gym in Siem Reap.
Minister Mam Bunheng continued, "In the last two decades, Cambodia has made significant progress in improving the health and nutrition of women of childbearing age, mothers, infants and young children." The maternal mortality rate decreased from 442 per 100,000 live births in 2005 to 170 in 2014, while the under-five mortality rate also decreased from 83 per 1,000 live births per year. 2000 to 35 cases in 2014.
Minister Mam Bunheng stated, "Although Cambodia has made great strides in improving the health of mothers and babies, malnutrition in Cambodia remains a priority for the Royal Government and its partners." All development. "Every year, nearly a third, or about 4,500 total child deaths in Cambodia, are linked to malnutrition."
Indeed, the promotion of breastfeeding has been hugely successful and historic for Cambodia. According to the results of three Cambodian Demographic and Health Surveys, exclusive breastfeeding for children aged 0-6 months increased from 11% in 2000 to 60% per year. 2005 and will continue to increase to 73.5% in 2010.
In addition, bottle-feeding increased rapidly from 11.8% in 2005 to 24.7% in 2010 and increased to 31% in 2014. Of particular concern is that one in three women who give birth at a private clinic feeds their newborn baby with breast milk substitutes.
In addition, breast milk is the perfect food for babies and children of all ages. Scientific evidence shows that breastfeeding immediately during the first hours after birth can prevent infant mortality by about 20% of children who are exclusively breastfed for the first six months. Reduced diarrhea 11 times and pneumonia 15 times compared to children who were not breastfed.
Failure to breastfeed can seriously affect a child's brain and memory development. Children who were not fully breastfed had smaller brain sizes than children who were fully breastfed. Not only that, they lost intelligence or IQ by 2.6 points compared to children who received full breastfeeding.
The Ministry of Health's Roadmap for Accelerating Nutrition Improvement (2014-2020) emphasizes that communication for behavioral change focuses on the first 1,000 golden days of a child's life, especially breastfeeding. Important among the eight components for accelerating progress in responding to malnutrition in mothers, infants and young children.
Minister Mam Bunheng called on all stakeholders working in maternal and child health and nutrition to continue the following activities:
1) Strengthen cooperation to ensure nutrition for mothers and children, in which breastfeeding is the best investment to reduce child malnutrition
2) - Continuing the education campaign to promote the importance of breastfeeding at both the national and sub-national levels
3) - Continue to encourage mothers to check the pregnancy before delivery and delivery at the hospital or health center.
4) -Continue to provide health services and care for maternal and child health through the expansion of hospitals and baby-loving communities throughout the country.
5) Public and private media must continue to promote and actively develop programs related to nutrition and breastfeeding with meaningful and educational content.
6) - Continue to strengthen the strict implementation of Sub-Decree No. 133 and Inter-Ministerial Joint Prakas No. 061 on the promotion and sale of products for feeding babies and children.
7) - Continue to monitor to strictly control the advertising and marketing of dairy products, powdered formulas that lead to confusion to the public.
