Samdech Techo Hun Sen, on the occasion of inspecting the construction site of the "Techo Heritage" National Stadium and meeting with workers, leaders, sports officials and many other athletes, also claimed that Samdech himself also eats pork every day. And never had a problem.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen said, "Regarding the African swine fever epidemic, I would like to call on our people to eat pork, do not worry, in addition to the affected areas that are banned and dead pigs that are buried in other areas. No side effects. "I also eat pork every day. There is no transmission from animals to humans."
According to the Prime Minister, in recent weeks, pork traders have complained that pork sales have been steadily declining following the outbreak of African swine fever. Ask people to suspend or reduce their consumption of pork.
In addition to calling for people to return to eating pork as usual, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia also explained some of the difficulties that affect the market, that is, if pork is not eaten, it leads to beef and fish. Rising prices, while this is also the season to ban fishing.
The Prime Minister also pointed out that the dead pigs infected with African swine fever were not allowed to be traded, and that the relevant ministries and institutions were required to reserve them in order to avoid the spread of the disease. Go to the other pigs nearby.
According to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the area where the African swine fever outbreak in Cambodia, compared to the rest of the country, is only a small area and can be controlled by the competent authorities to prevent the spread.
In connection with the outbreak of African swine fever, the relevant ministries have put in place a series of measures to prevent the spread of the disease. A total of 798 infected pigs were burnt, including 600 in Ratanakkiri, 64 in Tbong Khmum, 23 in Svay Rieng and 111 in Takeo.
African swine fever does not pose a risk to human health, but it does pose a threat to domestic and wild pigs, as the virus can live longer in processed pork, dried pork and pork. There is currently no cure for the disease and there is no vaccine or effective treatment yet, so when pigs become infected, the only measure is to destroy the affected herd.
