Association of Young Wat Scholars Students Association, Battambang Branch, Summarizing Meeting on Progress and Work Activities 2018-2019
(Battambang): This morning, July 27, 2019, the Battambang Branch of Young Scholars Students Association held a meeting to review the report on the progress and work activities of the association 2018-2019 and to continue the direction at Wat Po Veal Hall, Battambang City.
The ceremony was held in the presence of Mr. Heng Chumnit, President of the Association of Young Scholars, Wat Branch, Battambang Branch, and with the participation of the President, Vice President of the 13 sub-branches, districts and a total of more than 200 members of the association in the province.
Mr. Heng Chumnit said that under the constant guidance and guidance of Dr. Ming Ban Kosal, President of the Association of Young Wat Scholars Students, the Battambang Provincial Association of Young Scholars Students Association has always carried out its humanitarian work in the national interest. .
The purpose of this activity is to: 1: Unite and unite among all intellectual students 2: Protect the interests and rights of intellectual students before the law 3: To promote academic work for intellectual students to grow 4: Raise value Culture, civilization and sports 5: Improve the level of education and living standards of intellectual students.
"The members of our association are from all walks of life, including monks, intellectuals, government officials, students, philanthropists, young people living in pagodas (students) and some people," he said.
Mr. Heng Chumnit continued that after the structure of the association, the leaders and members of the association have always carried out large-scale humanitarian activities by visiting the pagoda members and the general public who are experiencing difficulties and lack of patients. The poor, orphans, the elderly and other victims, as well as other social work.
He added that as a young member of the pagoda, he came to do this work by sacrificing his personal resources, doing it willingly, not to work for the position and to seek religious gain, but to serve humanitarian work and social work as a whole.
On that occasion, the President gave alms to 4 monks: 100 kg of rice, 2 boxes of noodles, 2 bottles of Vital water, 4 cans of soy sauce, 4 sticks and suffixes, 50,000 Riels per person, and gave lunch at the pagoda to the members. All too.
