Washington: The State Department has reiterated that Washington will continue to resolve the issue through diplomatic means with North Korea, even as Pyongyang test-fired two short-range missiles on Thursday, as Donald Trump said. North Korea does not seem to consider the resumption of missile launches today as a big deal. This was released by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Friday afternoon, July 26, 2019.
North Korea and the United States are expected to begin talks in mid-July. But Pyongyang's resumption of missile tests is confusing the talks, as North Korean officials have warned they could cancel talks if joint US-South Korean military exercises take place. As planned in August.
However, in an interview with Fox News, Donald Trump did not consider North Korea's missile test a provocative act and even claimed that Washington and Pyongyang were achieving success.
"I have a good relationship with Kim Jong Un," Trump said. But we will see that happen. Punishment continued and hostages were released. They did not test nuclear weapons, nor did they test large ballistic missiles other than firing surface-to-air missiles. "But I think North Korea and the United States are achieving a lot."
According to the South Korean military, one of the two missiles North Korea test-fired Thursday morning was the latest in a series of new missiles ever seen. The missile was fired from the eastern coastal city of Wonsan, flying 690 kilometers before crashing into the Sea of Japan.
In addition, on Friday, North Korean leader Kim Jong Un even claimed that the test of the new missile was a warning message to some war-loving South Koreans. Although the name of the United States is mentioned, some experts believe that it is also a warning message to the United States. General Hun Manet was warmly welcomed by the Second Minister of Defense of Brunei Darussalam
