In response to this disaster, the national team visited the province, with the support of the National Committee for Disaster Management, philanthropists, as well as in cooperation with the provincial branch of the Red Cross and the Cambodian Red Cross, visited and distributed gifts to the families of the victims. Just in time.
After discussing the activities and experiences of the Disaster Management Committee at some local levels, Senior Minister Kun Kim praised the leadership of the provincial administration for its diligent response in the event of a disaster. Disasters happen and there are better preparedness plans.
On that occasion, through Senior Minister Kun Kim, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, sent greetings to government officials, the Armed Forces, authorities at all levels and people in the entire province.
Mr. Eang Sophalleth, Samdech Techo Hun Sen's personal assistant, told the media after the meeting that Mr. Hong Bo expressed his deep gratitude to Samdech Techo Hun Sen for giving his delegation the opportunity to pay a courtesy call and To find out about the agricultural situation in Cambodia.
On this occasion, he respectfully asked Samdech Techo Hun Sen to find out about the agricultural situation in Cambodia, because tomorrow he will go to Kandal province to check the agricultural situation in Cambodia.
The Prime Minister added that the failure factor is due to the first: countries in the ASEAN region is producing rice for export as well, second: Cambodia's production is still high. In this regard, Samdech also asked IFAD to review and share existing experiences and research to share with Cambodia. Other concerns include the growing Cambodian population, which is increasingly demanding land for agriculture.
In this regard, Samdech Techo Hun Sen informed the delegation that Cambodia is making sure that farmers are cultivating on land that is suitable for agricultural crops that can get high yields and promote the implementation of intensive cultivation. Plant to get the most out of existing land without having to expand the area.
The head of the Royal Government of Cambodia also briefed the delegation on Cambodia's concerns about climate change, which requires research into ways to adapt the species to climate change.
Regarding Cambodia's economic growth, Samdech Techo Hun Sen informed the delegates that Cambodia set four priorities in 1985: water, electricity and human resources, but now this priority has changed to water and electricity. On the other hand, this change is after many developments, especially the connection from one area to another of rural Cambodia.
In this sense, Cambodia has been able to sustain economic growth of about 7 percent over the past 20 years, thanks to connectivity and equal distribution of economic benefits to rural areas everywhere.
