North Korea's gross domestic product (GDP) fell another 4.1 percent in 2018, down from 3.5 percent in 2017, the report said. Given this figure, it is the largest drop since 1997, when the international community imposed sanctions on key North Korean sectors.
North Korea has never released public statistics on its annual economic growth, but South Korea's National Bank has been closely monitoring the North Korean economy since 1991. By collecting data from as many places as possible and taking it from its trading agencies that operate directly with North Korea.
The report also showed that trade between North Korea and the international community contracted to 48.4% in 2018, as sanctions at the end of 2016 and beyond in 2017 cut off exports. Nearly 90% of North Korea goes abroad. The export market for minerals was down 17.8 percent, while the forestry and fisheries sector was down 1.8 percent due to the 2018 drought.
