Swiss company expresses willingness to help develop technical and vocational training in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): A large Swiss company with branches in 70 countries has expressed its intention to assist the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training in the development of education, technical and vocational training (TVET) and other sectors such as industry. Tourism, energy, mining, agriculture with the Royal Government of Cambodia.
This expression of willingness to assist the Royal Government of Cambodia seems to highlight the prospects for achieving further goals in the policy of "Modernization of Education, Technical and Vocational Training" in Cambodia.
Mr. Anshul Sonak expressed his intention to assist the Ministry of Labor in the development of employment and vocational training, as well as to assist the Royal Government of Cambodia in the development of other sectors such as industry, tourism, energy, mining, agriculture, health and the mobilization of financial resources. Original.
He said that in the past, his company has worked in these areas in about 20 countries, such as Switzerland, the United States, the United Kingdom and other countries in Asia, Africa and Europe, with successful results.
He added that the company wants to help develop TVET skills in technical and vocational training institutions in Cambodia, but does not know if the Ministry wants his company to help in other areas, so asked for guidance from the Minister to prepare. Further related projects.
The Minister also pointed out the support for the modernization of TVET in Cambodia, because in the current industrial development in Cambodia, the inflow of foreign investors is also increasing, causing Cambodia to meet. Lack of skilled and technical manpower.
That is why the Minister wants the company to cooperate with the Ministry of Labor to carry out this work, because in the world, everyone knows that Switzerland is a country where everyone trusts the technical quality through many famous products. Made to the standards of this country.
However, the minister requested a thorough discussion with stakeholders before embarking on any development projects.
