The two countries have previously levied $ 360 billion in trade tariffs on each other, and Trump has threatened to impose more tariffs on Chinese goods. South Asia monsoon death toll rises to 669
South Asia: The death toll from natural disasters caused by the rainy season in South Asia has risen to 669 as of Tuesday, with rain expected to continue. More soaking. This is according to a quote from the Chinese newspaper CGTN on Wednesday, July 24, 2019.
At least 467 people have been killed in India since the beginning of July. In Nepal, 98 people were killed, in Bangladesh at least 78 people were killed and in Pakistan 26 people were killed. In addition to the dead, about 170 people were missing, along with hundreds or possibly thousands injured, as well as many other casualties. Rainfall is forecast to continue until August, especially in India.
During 2017, heavy rains during the rainy season caused floods, landslides and lightning that killed at least 1,200 people in India, Nepal, Pakistan and Bangladesh, killing 41 million people. Living in South Asia has been severely affected.
General Department of Financial Industry holds a meeting to discuss and manage the challenges of the mortgage business
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Mey Van, Royal Government Delegate in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Financial Industry, on July 24, 2019, chaired a meeting to discuss the management and resolution of all challenges related to the mortgage business. Possessed by a small grant that the Ministry has delegated to the Capital-Provincial Departments of Economy and Finance.
The meeting was also attended by representatives of the Ministry of Commerce, representatives of the General Secretariat of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, representatives of the General Department of Taxation, representatives of the General Department of the National Treasury and representatives of the Capital-Provincial Departments of Economy and Finance throughout the country.
As a result of today's meeting, a number of inter-institutional challenges were addressed, especially on business registration, revenue registration, business licenses and tax obligations.
Mr. Mey Van added that now the mortgage market is growing day by day, so it requires us, as regulators and guardians, to manage clearly, especially to provide better public services, leaving the people Citizens are partners and good friends.
At the same time, the General Department of Financial Industry has set a guideline for the meeting to pay attention to any businesses that do not yet have proper laws and need to take urgent measures to prevent this problem effectively, especially in some provinces. Who are not yet active in this work.
Finally, he strongly hopes that after this important meeting, the whole meeting will bring the guidelines set out in this direction to implement effectively and efficiently.
